\description{
Computes regional consistency probabilities (RCPs) for a multi-regional
clinical trial (MRCT) with a time-to-event primary endpoint analysed by a
group sequential design (GSD). Regional consistency is evaluated with
Method 1 and Method 2 of the Japanese Ministry of Health, Labour and
Welfare (MHLW, 2007) guidance, either on the log hazard ratio scale
(Ikeda and Bretz, 2010; Homma, 2024) or on the hazard reduction scale
\eqn{1 - \mathrm{HR}} (Hayashi and Itoh, 2018).

Because consistency is evaluated at the analysis where the trial stops
for efficacy, the relevant quantity is the RCP conditional on stopping
for efficacy at analysis \eqn{k}. It is obtained in closed form as a
ratio of multivariate normal probabilities, using the canonical joint
distribution of the overall and regional treatment effect estimates
across analyses.
}
\details{
\strong{Model.} Let \eqn{\delta_s = -\log(\mathrm{HR}_s)} denote the true
treatment effect in region \eqn{s = 1, \ldots, S} (region 1 is the region
of interest, e.g., Japan), \eqn{d_k} the cumulative number of events at
analysis \eqn{k = 1, \ldots, K}, and \eqn{f_{s,k}} the proportion of these
events that occur in region \eqn{s}. With allocation ratio \eqn{r}, the
regional information is \eqn{I_{s,k} = r f_{s,k} d_k / (1 + r)^2}
(Schoenfeld approximation). The regional estimates
\eqn{\hat{\delta}_{s,k}} are normally distributed with mean
\eqn{\delta_s} and variance \eqn{1 / I_{s,k}}, have independent
increments within a region and are independent across regions. The
overall estimate and Wald statistic are
\eqn{\hat{\delta}_k = \sum_s w_{s,k} \hat{\delta}_{s,k}} with
\eqn{w_{s,k} = I_{s,k} / I_k}, and \eqn{Z_k = \hat{\delta}_k \sqrt{I_k}}.
Because \eqn{f_{s,k}} may vary with \eqn{k}, delayed enrolment in the
region of interest can be represented.

\strong{Consistency criteria} at analysis \eqn{k}, with
\eqn{\widehat{\mathrm{HR}} = \exp(-\hat{\delta})}:
\itemize{
\item Method 1, \code{scale = "loghr"}:
\eqn{\hat{\delta}_{1,k} > \gamma_{M1} \hat{\delta}_k}.
\item Method 1, \code{scale = "hr"}:
\eqn{1 - \widehat{\mathrm{HR}}_{1,k} > \gamma_{M1}
    (1 - \widehat{\mathrm{HR}}_k)}.
\item Method 2, \code{scale = "loghr"}:
\eqn{\hat{\delta}_{s,k} > \gamma_{M2}} for all \eqn{s}.
\item Method 2, \code{scale = "hr"}:
\eqn{1 - \widehat{\mathrm{HR}}_{s,k} > \gamma_{M2}} for all \eqn{s}.
}
With the default \code{gamma_M2 = 0}, both scales give the same Method 2
criterion.

\strong{Probabilities reported.} Let
\eqn{E_k = \{l_j < Z_j < u_j, j < k; Z_k \ge u_k\}} denote stopping for
efficacy at analysis \eqn{k}, with efficacy boundaries \eqn{u_k} and
futility boundaries \eqn{l_k}, and let \eqn{C_k} denote the consistency
event at analysis \eqn{k}. For each analysis the function reports
\eqn{\Pr(E_k)} (\code{p_eff}), \eqn{\Pr(E_k \cap C_k)} (\code{p_joint}),
the conditional RCP \eqn{\Pr(C_k \mid E_k)} (\code{rcp}) and the
unconditional probability \eqn{\Pr(C_k)} (\code{rcp_uncond}), which is
what the fixed-design formula would give if applied to the data
available at analysis \eqn{k}. The overall RCP is
\eqn{\sum_k \Pr(E_k \cap C_k) / \sum_k \Pr(E_k)}, the probability of
consistency given that the trial is successful.

For Method 1 on the log hazard ratio scale and for Method 2, all
probabilities are multivariate normal probabilities. For Method 1 on the
hazard ratio scale, \code{hr_method = "exact"} conditions on the regional
estimate and integrates numerically (Gauss-Legendre quadrature for the
joint probability, \code{stats::integrate()} for the unconditional
probability), whereas \code{hr_method = "delta"} linearizes the criterion
by the delta method as in Hayashi and Itoh (2018).

\strong{Binding and non-binding futility boundaries.} Binding futility
boundaries are always applied. For non-binding futility boundaries,
\code{futility = "obey"} (default) assumes that the trial stops when a
futility boundary is crossed, and \code{futility = "ignore"} assumes that
it never stops for futility (the assumption under which non-binding
efficacy boundaries are calculated).

\strong{Design input.} The efficacy and futility boundaries are taken
from \code{design} as they are; they are not recalculated for the
information fractions implied by \code{events}. A warning is issued if
\code{events / events[K]} differs from the planned information fractions
of \code{design} by more than 0.01.
}
\examples{
# Three analyses with O'Brien-Fleming-type boundaries and a binding
# futility boundary; Japan (region 1) enrols late
design <- list(upper   = c(3.7103, 2.5114, 1.9930),
               lower   = c(0, 0.5),
               binding = TRUE,
               alpha   = 0.025)
fs <- cbind(c(0.06, 0.47, 0.47),
            c(0.09, 0.455, 0.455),
            c(0.10, 0.45, 0.45))
res <- rcpgsd(design, events = c(150, 300, 450), fs = fs, hr = 0.72)
print(res)

# Hazard reduction scale (Hayashi and Itoh, 2018), exact evaluation
res_hr <- rcpgsd(design, events = c(150, 300, 450), fs = fs, hr = 0.72,
                 method = "M1", scale = "hr")
print(res_hr)

# Fixed design (single analysis) for comparison
res_fix <- rcpgsd(list(upper = qnorm(0.975)), events = 450,
                  fs = c(0.10, 0.45, 0.45), hr = 0.72)
print(res_fix)

\donttest{
# Boundaries from gsDesign (non-binding futility)
if (requireNamespace("gsDesign", quietly = TRUE)) {
  gsd <- gsDesign::gsDesign(k = 3, test.type = 4, alpha = 0.025,
                            beta = 0.1, sfu = gsDesign::sfLDOF,
                            sfl = gsDesign::sfHSD, sflpar = -2)
  res_gs <- rcpgsd(gsd, events = c(150, 300, 450),
                   fs = c(0.10, 0.45, 0.45), hr = 0.72,
                   futility = "ignore")
  print(res_gs)
}
}

